f note names for "Twinkle Twinkle" in every key! But with movable do, you only learn one: "Do - Do - Sol - Sol - La - La - Sol". You can play it and sing it anywhere. Movable Do makes ear training and sight-reading *much* easier because it allows you to learn the patterns that make up music. So, in essence, fixed do tells you *where* the notes are physically located (which keys you press on an instrument), while movable do tells you *how* the notes sound and function within a key, which also helps you understand their relationship. It's not an unnecessary complication; it's a simplification, a tool that helps us understand the *language* of music beyond just its individual notes.
