 is the Assistant (Sol). In music, notes have distinct "jobs" depending on the key you are in. * **"Do"** is the home base. It feels resolved and peaceful. * **"Ti"** is the tension note. If you sing *Do-Re-Mi-Fa-Sol-La-Ti...* and stop, your brain practically screams for you to resolve it back to *Do*. Movable Do trains musicians to hear the *function* of a note. When a singer sees a "Ti" on a piece of sheet music, they know exactly what it's supposed to feel and sound like (tension), regardless of whether that note happens to be a B, an F#, or a C#. ### Summary * **Letters (C, D, E):** Tell you exactly where to put your fingers on the piano or guitar. (Absolute pitch). * **Movable Do (Do, Re, Mi):** Tells your brain and your voice how the song is structured, how it feels, and how the notes relate to each other. (Relative pitch). So, while having two systems seems redundant at first, they are actually doing two completely different jobs. Letters handle the mechanics, while Movable Do handles the math and the melody!
